"name":"California Institute of Technology","location":"Pasadena, California, United States"}},"id":"bde45c6c108d407faa6b503281f7ee07","userType":"OTHER"},{"organizationId":"53479116edca5eee9321c29119d8effb","sequence":9,"submissionId":20205008958,"meta":{"author":{"name":"R H Hoover"},"organization":{"name":"Southwest Research Institute","location":"San Antonio, Texas, United States"}},"id":"570733888f2a4bcfa3dbc025f6d4d81c","userType":"OTHER"},{"organizationId":"9da3c67e960a5904bf41d1840705ade5","sequence":10,"submissionId":20205008958,"meta":{"author":{"orcidId":"0000-0002-4331-913X","name":"I B Smith"},"organization":{"name":"Planetary Science Institute","location":"Tucson, Arizona, United States"}},"id":"22eaa68e6cb3466dafda629fb4fcf3cc","userId":"34e476f0bbca478fa66393bbc9c6c88e"},{"organizationId":"9da3c67e960a5904bf41d1840705ade5","sequence":11,"submissionId":20205008958,"meta":{"author":{"name":"A Pathare"},"organization":{"name":"Planetary Science Institute","location":"Tucson, Arizona, United States"}},"id":"7ad9061f3d6742f0bb0214e353bde945","userType":"OTHER"},{"sequence":12,"submissionId":20205008958,"meta":{"author":{"orcidId":"0000-0003-2343-7224","name":"C M Dundas"},"organization":{"name":"U.S. Geological SurveyAstrogeology Science Center","location":"Flagstaff, AZ"}},"id":"5d03b630ee914be6b5da98eb2e040408","userId":"953922b9182149a8a02c02118e58b4db"},{"organizationId":"e072aa48cddc584595ffef7f06238b1a","sequence":13,"submissionId":20205008958,"meta":{"author":{"name":"B A Campbell"},"organization":{"name":"Smithsonian Institution","location":"Washington D.C., District of Columbia, United States"}},"id":"fca80999c5c54255bd76108a6e72dba1","userType":"OTHER"}],"stiTypeDetails":"Accepted Manuscript (Version with final changes)","technicalReviewType":"EXTERNAL_PEER_COMMITTEE","modified":"2025-08-31T18:39:21.7150190+00:00","id":20205008958,"created":"2020-10-20T18:31:26.8199100+00:00","center":{"code":"GSFC","name":"Goddard Space Flight Center","id":"973fae00c3584b9fb4c762ae9870d0f6"},"onlyAbstract":false,"sensitiveInformation":2,"abstract":"Multiple nations and private entities are pushing to make landing humans on Mars a reality. The majority of proposed mission architectures envision “living off the land” by leveraging Martian water-ice deposits for fuel production and other purposes. Fortunately for mission designers, water ice exists on Mars in plentiful volumes. The challenge is isolating accessible ice deposits within regions that optimize other preferred  landing-site  conditions.  Here,  we  present  the first results  of  the  Mars  Subsurface Water  Ice Mapping (SWIM) project, which has the aim of searching for buried ice resources across the mid-latitudes. Through  the  integration  of  orbital  datasets  in  concert  with  new  data-processing  techniques,  the  SWIM project assesses the likelihood of ice by quantifying the consistency of multiple, independent data sources with the presence of ice. Concentrating our efforts across a significant portion of the northern Hemisphere, our composite ice consistency maps indicate that the broad plains of Arcadia and the extensive glacial networks across Deuteronilus Mensae match the greatest number of remote sensing criteria for accessible ice-rich, subsurface material situated equatorward of the contemporary ice-stability zone.","isLessonsLearned":false,"disseminated":"DOCUMENT_AND_METADATA","cui":{"isCui":false,"submissionId":20205008958,"id":"3eb8e8f1974047dd9e11edc58a4d1fea"},"meetings":[{"country":"US","submissionId":20205008958,"name":"","location":"","id":"455c3138344745e8b5bb9ce11b825d27"}],"publications":[{"volume":"5","submissionId":20205008958,"publisher":"Springer Nature\t","eissn":"2397-3366","id":"80179a0a5bc94b8ca7eb5b6490c2b28f","publicationName":"Nature Astronomy","publicationDate":"2021-02-08T05:00:00.0000000+00:00","issuePublicationDate":"2021-01-01T05:00:00.0000000+00:00","doi":"10.1038/s41550-020-01290-z"}],"status":"CURATED","related":[]